# Line endings, settled once so they cannot drift.
#
# Divixi is developed on Windows and the repository is public, so a macOS or
# Linux contributor can arrive at any time. Without this file, what lands in a
# commit depends on each person's `core.autocrlf`, and the day someone has it
# set to `true` a whole file turns over in a diff that says nothing.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what text looks like
# in the repository. Working copies are unaffected on Unix, and on Windows the
# editors this project uses all handle LF.
#
# This costs nothing to adopt: every text file here is already LF. Checked when
# it was added -- the only tracked files containing a CR byte were 50 PNGs, one
# ICO and one ICNS, which is image data rather than a line ending, and
# `git add --renormalize .` restaged nothing. So this records what is already
# true rather than rewriting the tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, stated rather than guessed.
#
# `text=auto` asks Git to sniff each file, and a heuristic that is right almost
# always is still a heuristic. A PNG misread as text would be rewritten on
# checkout and arrive corrupt. These say so outright.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

# Lockfiles are generated, not written.
#
# `linguist-generated` collapses them in a pull request by default -- a
# dependency bump is then a readable manifest change rather than a thousand
# lines of resolved tree. They stay expandable, and `git diff` is untouched:
# when the resolved versions are the thing being checked, they are still there.
Cargo.lock linguist-generated
package-lock.json linguist-generated
